                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2853692,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "One study compared people's enjoyment of dancing at home in front of a video with dancing in a group in a studio. The people dancing in a group reported feeling happier, whereas those dancing alone did not. In another experiment, university researchers at York and Sheffield took a group of students and sent each of them into a lab where music was played for five minutes. Each had to choose from three options, to sit and listen quietly to the music, to cycle on an exercise bike while they listened, or to get up and dance. All were given cognitive tasks to perform before and after.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2853693,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "One study compared people's enjoyment of dancing at home in front of a video with dancing in a group in a studio.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 121.2,
                  "from": 112.799995,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2853716,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "The people dancing in a group reported feeling happier, whereas those dancing alone did not.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 129.095,
                  "from": 121.975,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2853732,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "In another experiment, university researchers at York and Sheffield took a group of students and sent each of them into a lab where music was played for five minutes.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 142.45001,
                  "from": 130.455,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2853762,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Each had to choose from three options, to sit and listen quietly to the music, to cycle on an exercise bike while they listened, or to get up and dance.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 156.02501,
                  "from": 143.09,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2853793,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "All were given cognitive tasks to perform before and after.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 161.785,
                  "from": 156.825,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2853804,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "The result showed that those who chose to dance showed much more creativity when doing problem solving tasks. Doctor Lovett at the University of Hertfordshire believes dance could be a very useful way to help people suffering from mental health problems. He thinks dance should be prescribed as therapy to help people overcome issues such as depression. It's well established that dance is a good way of encouraging adolescent girls to take exercise, but what about older people? Studies have shown that there are enormous benefits for people in their sixties and beyond.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2853805,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "The result showed that those who chose to dance showed much more creativity when doing problem solving tasks.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 171.77,
                  "from": 162.81,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2853824,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Doctor Lovett at the University of Hertfordshire believes dance could be a very useful way to help people suffering from mental health problems.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 183.30501,
                  "from": 173.21,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2853848,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "He thinks dance should be prescribed as therapy to help people overcome issues such as depression.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 190.825,
                  "from": 184.025,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2853865,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "It's well established that dance is a good way of encouraging adolescent girls to take exercise, but what about older people?",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 205.2,
                  "from": 196.0,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2853887,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Studies have shown that there are enormous benefits for people in their sixties and beyond.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 211.755,
                  "from": 206.155,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2853903,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "One of the great things about dance is that there are no barriers to participation. Anyone can have a go, even those whose standard of fitness is quite low. Dance can be especially beneficial for older adults who can't run or do more intense workouts, or for those who don't want to. One 2015 study found that even a gentle dance workout helps to promote a healthy heart. And there's plenty of evidence which suggests that dancing lowers the risk of falls, which could result in a broken hip, for example, by helping people to improve their balance.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2853904,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "One of the great things about dance is that there are no barriers to participation.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 217.755,
                  "from": 212.395,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2853920,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Anyone can have a go, even those whose standard of fitness is quite low.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 224.37,
                  "from": 218.61,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2853935,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Dance can be especially beneficial for older adults who can't run or do more intense workouts, or for those who don't want to.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 235.54501,
                  "from": 225.48999,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2853959,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "One 2015 study found that even a gentle dance workout helps to promote a healthy heart.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 244.425,
                  "from": 236.425,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2853976,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "And there's plenty of evidence which suggests that dancing lowers the risk of falls, which could result in a broken hip, for example, by helping people to improve their balance.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 258.04,
                  "from": 245.48001,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2854007,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "There are some less obvious benefits of dance for older people too. One thing I hadn't realized before researching this topic was that dance isn't just a physical challenge. It also requires a lot of concentration, because you need to remember different steps and routines. For older people, this kind of activity is especially important because it forces their brain to process things more quickly and to retain more information. Current research also shows that dance promotes a general sense of well-being in older participants, which can last up to a week after a class.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2854008,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "There are some less obvious benefits of dance for older people too.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 264.73502,
                  "from": 260.01498,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2854021,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "One thing I hadn't realized before researching this topic was that dance isn't just a physical challenge.",
